      "transcript": "Mae Solway: For a fair comparison, every vessel needs the same dimensions. A larger pot heats and cools differently, so size would otherwise confound the effect of the temper.\nDorian Kells: We can use moulds for the rim diameter and height, then measure again after drying because the mixtures may shrink differently.\nMae Solway: What should count as the control group?\nDorian Kells: Pots made from the same clay but with no added temper. They must receive the same mixing, drying, handling and firing schedule as the experimental pots.\nMae Solway: If a pot seems too close to the hottest zone once the fire is burning, should we move it?\nDorian Kells: No. Movement would introduce uncontrolled temperature variation and might cause damage itself. We’ll randomise positions before ignition and record the layout on a scaled sketch.\nMae Solway: Would a second firing the following weekend give us replication?\nDorian Kells: Not reliable replication. Weather and fuel conditions could not be held constant, so a second day might add a new confound. It could be analysed as a separate experiment, but not casually merged with this one.\nMae Solway: Then the main improvement over our first design is to place witness tiles around the firing area. They provide spatial evidence without disturbing the pots.\nDorian Kells: For the measurement sheet, keep wall thickness similar. Thick walls may survive because they warm more slowly, not because a temper recipe is better.\nMae Solway: Any vessel outside the agreed thickness or size tolerance should be recorded and excluded before the firing rather than moved to a smaller group.\nDorian Kells: We’ll also record the fuel mass before firing. A weighed amount is more defensible than saying that each wood pile looked roughly equal.\nMae Solway: During the firing, one person will log wind direction at fixed intervals. Changes in airflow can alter oxygen supply and push heat across the firing area.\nDorian Kells: Humidity will be measured once before ignition as background context, not entered in the repeated firing log. That keeps the requested series unambiguous.\nMae Solway: The witness tiles should go at measured distances and orientations around the fire. Their colour and surface changes will give us a rough map of heat exposure between the three temperature sensors.\nDorian Kells: After cooling, classify vessel damage by fracture pattern rather than simply calling pots broken or unbroken. Photograph fragments in place and record where each crack began.\nMae Solway: Each pot will receive a code that hides its temper recipe from the person scoring cracks. Obvious colour differences may remain, but blinding can still reduce selective attention.\nDorian Kells: We should define the fracture categories before firing and practise them on discarded examples. The report must include excluded pots and missing measurements, not only successful specimens.\nMae Solway: Photographs, logs and sensor files also need synchronised times. Then a sudden wind change or fuel addition can be compared with later damage without relying on memory.\nDorian Kells: Finally, reserve clay for chemical and microscopic work. Specimens must be selected by a written rule rather than because they look dramatic; otherwise unusual damage could dominate the conclusion.\nMae Solway: For each vessel, the record should link the recipe, dimensions, coded position and photograph without revealing the recipe to the first damage scorer. A second researcher can unlock the codes only after the initial classifications are frozen.\nDorian Kells: We also need a rule for missing data. If a sensor fails, we should flag that interval rather than infer a temperature from nearby readings. The witness tiles offer spatial context, but they are not calibrated thermometers and should not be presented as substitutes.\nMae Solway: When the fire is fully cold, fragments from each pot will go onto a separate tray before transport. Mixing pieces from neighbouring vessels would destroy the connection between a fracture and its firing position.\nDorian Kells: The final comparison will therefore combine survival, crack origin, deformation and surface change. One recipe may reduce catastrophic breakage yet produce severe warping, so ranking pots by a single outcome would conceal an important trade-off. We will present the coded observations before interpreting recipes, keep the raw photographs alongside the scoring table, and ask a second scorer to classify a sample independently. Agreement will show whether the categories are usable; disagreement will be resolved by revisiting the written criteria rather than by choosing the more convenient outcome.",
