      "passage": "A\nA line-casting machine began with a keyboard, but pressing a key did not print a letter. It released a small brass matrix from a magazine above the operator. Each matrix carried the recessed mould for one character. The selected matrices travelled down channels and gathered in sequence at the assembler, where the operator could inspect the line before casting. This separation between selection and printing was central: the keyboard organised reusable moulds rather than striking inked type directly onto paper. Magazines held multiple fonts and sizes, so the operator selected the appropriate bank before beginning. Copy was marked with type instructions, and a wrong selection could require several lines to be reset even when every word had been keyed correctly.\n\nB\nA line had to fill a prescribed width even when its words did not do so naturally. The operator inserted wedge-shaped spacebands between words. Once the matrices reached the correct sequence, the machine expanded those spacebands until the row fitted the measure. The spaces, not the letters, absorbed the extra width. This mechanical justification produced a straight right-hand edge while preserving the chosen type size, although poor spacing decisions could still leave an unattractive page. Operators judged spacing while reading ahead. A line with too many wide gaps was often reset rather than accepted merely because it reached the measure. Thus mechanical expansion depended on typographic judgement about what readers would find tolerable.\n\nC\nAt the casting station, molten type metal was forced against the aligned matrices to form one solid line, known as a slug. After cooling, the slug moved to a tray for page assembly, while the matrices were lifted away for reuse. Casting an entire line reduced the handling of individual pieces of type, but accuracy still mattered: a wrong matrix embedded an error in metal and usually required the whole line to be cast again. The moulding cycle was rapid, but the metal needed a controlled composition and temperature. Metal that was too cool might not fill fine details; excessive heat increased wear and made the working environment more hazardous. Rejected slugs returned to the melting pot.\n\nD\nMechanisation changed compositors’ work without removing skill. Keyboard operators had to read copy rapidly, anticipate line length and notice errors before casting. Machinists diagnosed jams, controlled metal temperature and adjusted alignment. Proofreaders then compared the cast text with the manuscript. The technology redistributed expertise among specialised roles; it did not turn composition into an automatic process requiring no judgement. Speed and accuracy were measured together. An operator who produced many lines but forced frequent recasting could delay the page more than a slower colleague whose copy required little correction. Training therefore included rhythm, abbreviation conventions and the diagnosis of recurring faults.\n\nE\nAfter casting, the matrices rose to a distributor. Their coded teeth differed by character, and a rotating mechanism carried each matrix along the distribution channel until the pattern matched the entrance to its own magazine slot. It then dropped into place for reuse. A damaged or incorrectly coded matrix could enter the wrong channel and later produce a puzzling substitution, so cleaning and inspection were part of routine operation. Distribution allowed the same matrices to circulate repeatedly during a shift. It was one reason the machine could sustain newspaper production without storing a separate piece of type for every occurrence of a common letter.\n\nF\nThe composing room combined speed with hazards. Operators worked beside molten metal, hot surfaces and moving mechanisms, while fumes and metal dust required ventilation. Noise made conversation difficult, and a jam could tempt an impatient worker to reach into machinery. Safe production therefore depended on guards, disciplined maintenance and the handling of metal residues as carefully as on typing speed. Modern demonstrations use barriers and safer materials because historical practice is not an acceptable safety standard. Ventilation also protected the quality of casting by controlling heat around the machine. Safety and output were intertwined: neglected cleaning increased both mechanical faults and workers’ exposure to residues.\n\nG\nPhotocomposition and later digital systems eventually replaced line casting in most newspaper plants. Text could be stored, corrected and arranged without producing a metal slug for every line, shortening revision and removing much hot-metal handling. Adoption was gradual rather than instantaneous: firms often ran old and new systems together while staff learned new processes and managers tested whether deadlines and page assembly remained reliable. The change reorganised whole production schedules, not merely the operator’s keyboard. Some occupations disappeared, others narrowed, and new work developed in photographic processing, electronic typesetting and system maintenance. Describing the transition simply as deskilling misses that uneven redistribution.",
