      "passage": "A\nAn internal prediction market lets employees trade contracts tied to future events, such as whether a project will meet a deadline. The changing price summarises participants’ willingness to back an outcome. Supporters value the mechanism because it can aggregate dispersed information that staff may hesitate to voice in meetings. A technician who sees a supply problem and a salesperson who notices weak demand can both affect the estimate without first producing a single agreed narrative. Contracts can also be designed around sales, regulatory approval or completion of a technical milestone. Each captures a different uncertainty, so combining them into one broad question can hide which part of a project participants doubt.\n\nB\nA market price is not automatically true. Participation may be thin, incentives may be weak, and several traders may rely on the same mistaken source. Protected anonymity can reduce pressure from organisational rank, allowing a junior employee to express a pessimistic view, but it cannot create independent evidence. Design and culture determine whether a price reflects broad knowledge or merely the confidence of a small group. Correlated information is a particular danger when many employees rely on the same dashboard or manager. Numerous trades may then create an appearance of consensus without adding independent observations.\n\nC\nContracts must describe a specified event and date. ‘The product launches by 30 June’ can be settled mechanically; ‘the project is successful’ cannot, unless success has already been defined. Precise wording prevents organisers from changing the meaning after forecasts are made and makes disputes easier to resolve. It also tells participants what evidence is relevant instead of asking them to price a vague aspiration. Settlement sources should be named in advance—for example, a signed acceptance record or a published launch date. Otherwise two honest participants may interpret the same event differently.\n\nD\nCredibility accumulates through visible settlement under stable rules. When an event occurs, the organisation should publish how the contract was resolved and pay or score it according to the rule announced at the start. Cancelling inconvenient questions or redefining deadlines teaches employees that conformity matters more than accuracy. A repeated record of fair settlement is therefore more important than an elaborate trading interface. Stable rules include a declared response to cancellation, postponement and missing evidence. Edge cases are not administrative trivia; unresolved ambiguity reduces willingness to trade in later rounds.\n\nE\nIncentives require balance. Virtual currency and modest prizes can widen participation without exposing employees to financial loss. Real stakes may focus attention in some settings, but they also introduce legal, ethical and equity concerns. The author does not claim that one arrangement always outperforms the other. What matters is whether the reward is sufficient to encourage considered forecasts without turning participation into gambling or excluding staff. Participation time is another cost. If trading requires constant attention, the market may privilege employees whose jobs allow frequent screen access rather than those with the best information.\n\nF\nFear can nullify the mechanism. If employees believe a pessimistic trade will damage their career, they may stay silent or imitate senior opinion. A market then reproduces the hierarchy it was meant to bypass. For this reason, a price is best treated as an early-warning signal: an unexpected movement should trigger interviews, checks of project data or scenario analysis, not an automatic managerial command. Confidential reporting channels may still be needed because a price shows that concern exists without revealing its factual basis. Investigation supplies the explanation that trading alone cannot provide.\n\nG\nLeaders must be willing to examine inconvenient results. They should compare the market with surveys, expert panels and later outcomes, recording where the methods disagree. Participation and liquidity should accompany any headline price because a precise number can conceal very few trades. Regular evaluation shows whether forecasts are calibrated and whether information reaches the market in time to influence a decision. Calibration should be examined across many contracts, not inferred from one celebrated success. Organisations also need to record forecasts that attracted little activity, since selective evaluation exaggerates performance.\n\nH\nThe conclusion is conditional. Internal markets can complement organisational decisions when questions are resolvable, participation is broad, settlement is credible and governance protects honest disagreement. Their usefulness declines where outcomes cannot be verified or fear suppresses participation. Procedures should name who reviews a signal, how conflicting evidence is recorded and when further investigation is required. The market earns authority through accountable performance over time, not through the market label or the visual precision of a price. A market should be discontinued when it repeatedly lacks participation or when settlement cannot be insulated from managerial discretion. Maintaining a visible number under those conditions adds theatre rather than evidence.",
